description:
Reads a crontab and proposes one monitor per scheduled line. Without apply=1 nothing is written; the proposal says what was ignored and why. Passwords in the command are redacted. The body is the crontab itself, as `text/plain`. Without `?apply=1` **nothing is written**: you get the proposal — what would become a monitor, what was ignored and why, and what still fits in the free tier. With `apply=1` it is all or nothing, under the same rules as `POST /api/endpoints`. Comments, variables and `@reboot` are ignored (there is no way to know when to expect a reboot); `@daily` and friends become the equivalent expression. The command becomes the monitor name, with passwords and tokens redacted first — the name travels in the alert e-mail, in the public dashboard and in the badge. Returns: { applied, recognized, ignored, existing, would_create, created?, free_tier }
Example: crontab -l | curl -s -XPOST "$ORIGIN/api/import/crontab?apply=1" -H "X-Guest-Token: $HP" -H "content-type: text/plain" --data-binary @-
Errors: 400: `crontab_sem_agenda` — no schedule line was recognised.; 401: No credential, or an invalid one. See this endpoint's auth.; 402: Payment required. The x402 terms (USDC on Base) come in the `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header and in `accepts[]`: pay and repeat the same call with `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` (x402 v2) or `X-PAYMENT` (v1). A standard x402 client such as `@x402/fetch` does this for you.
